WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:01.890
- I know, I love this panel today.

00:00:01.890 --> 00:00:03.060
Well everybody meet each other.

00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:03.893
Dame Helen.

00:00:03.893 --> 00:00:04.726
- Yeah. Hi.
- Hi.

00:00:04.726 --> 00:00:05.559
- Hi.
- Hi guys.

00:00:05.559 --> 00:00:06.900
- Well actually-
- Hey Adam.

00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:07.770
- You know Adam.

00:00:07.770 --> 00:00:08.603
- Yep. We do.
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:00:08.603 --> 00:00:10.470
I was like, you know,
'cause of Leighton Meester.

00:00:10.470 --> 00:00:11.340
If y'all didn't know, that's his wife.

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:13.293
So y'all know each other from when?

00:00:14.190 --> 00:00:15.343
- Gosh, I guess I've known-

00:00:15.343 --> 00:00:16.176
- Yeah. When did you guys meet?

00:00:16.176 --> 00:00:17.009
- When you did the movie together?

00:00:17.009 --> 00:00:18.540
- I guess, Leighton and
I did a movie together,

00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:20.550
called "The Roommate,"
when we were like 20,

00:00:20.550 --> 00:00:22.560
or I was 20 and we're close age.

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:23.393
So, yeah.
- All right.

00:00:23.393 --> 00:00:25.059
- [Kelly] Oh wow.
- So it's been a while, yeah.

00:00:25.059 --> 00:00:26.885
- Adam's put up with a lot of
girl nights. (Adam laughing)

00:00:26.885 --> 00:00:27.903
He's put up with a lot
of, he's a good guy.

00:00:27.903 --> 00:00:28.762
- [Kelly] You're invited
to the girl nights?

00:00:28.762 --> 00:00:29.618
- Yeah, you have some good (indistinct).

00:00:29.618 --> 00:00:30.701
- You're there?
- Well, he has to be there

00:00:30.701 --> 00:00:31.879
for the children right?

00:00:31.879 --> 00:00:33.425
- They come to me and then I just kind of-

00:00:33.425 --> 00:00:35.460
- Yeah, you're kind of like,
"Hey, you guys having fun?"

00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:36.293
Yeah.
- Yeah.

00:00:36.293 --> 00:00:37.959
(Kelly laughing)
- I love that you go,

00:00:37.959 --> 00:00:38.842
they come to me.
- They come to me.

00:00:38.842 --> 00:00:40.230
(people laughing)

00:00:40.230 --> 00:00:42.810
- It came out in a different
way and it was awesome.

00:00:42.810 --> 00:00:45.900
So you have a rule when
working together. What is it?

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:48.870
- Oh, we each have a CBA,
which we can pull at any time

00:00:48.870 --> 00:00:50.670
during the record making.
- What's a CBA?

00:00:50.670 --> 00:00:52.860
- It's a can't be argued card.
- Can't be argued.

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:55.200
- And we stole it from The
National. It's basically like-

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:56.033
- Can't be argued.
- Can't be argued.

00:00:56.033 --> 00:00:57.699
- You can't be argued.
- Oh, can't be argued card.

00:00:57.699 --> 00:00:58.890
- You can do this on set too.

00:00:58.890 --> 00:01:00.175
I would pull this as an actor too.

00:01:00.175 --> 00:01:01.008
- It can be in your contract.
- Yeah.

00:01:01.008 --> 00:01:02.370
- Basically a veto?

00:01:02.370 --> 00:01:03.203
- Yes.
- Yeah.

00:01:03.203 --> 00:01:05.190
- Okay.
- Or we use it the other way,

00:01:05.190 --> 00:01:06.090
opposite of a veto,

00:01:06.090 --> 00:01:08.040
where it's like, this
is my can't be argued,

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:10.470
this is my thing that if
you take this away from me,

00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:11.850
this is gonna not work for the band.

00:01:11.850 --> 00:01:13.410
Like, I need this moment.

00:01:13.410 --> 00:01:14.400
Let me have it.
- And you get one?

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:15.510
- You get one.
- You get one.

00:01:15.510 --> 00:01:16.740
- Oh, you only get one that's good.

00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:17.850
- We each get one.
- Because, you know-

00:01:17.850 --> 00:01:19.260
- Per album? Per day?

00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:20.304
- Per album.
- One per album.

00:01:20.304 --> 00:01:21.330
- Per album.
- Okay.

00:01:21.330 --> 00:01:22.710
- I like per day.
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:01:22.710 --> 00:01:25.017
- This is my CBA for today.
- Per hour.

00:01:25.017 --> 00:01:25.850
- Per hour.

00:01:25.850 --> 00:01:27.096
- [Kelly] There should be one per day.

00:01:27.096 --> 00:01:28.410
- There should. There should.

00:01:28.410 --> 00:01:32.160
- Has there ever been one
where the other has been like,

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:34.650
no, really, I don't think
this is a good idea?

00:01:34.650 --> 00:01:36.630
Where you really push,
you know, you're like,

00:01:36.630 --> 00:01:37.680
come on.
- Oh yeah.

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:39.180
We're usually on the same page

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:41.611
when it comes to joint
decisions, we really are.

00:01:41.611 --> 00:01:43.527
I don't know why, but we are.
- Yeah, we are.

00:01:43.527 --> 00:01:46.259
- Has someone ever pulled a
card and you've been like, no.

00:01:46.259 --> 00:01:47.092
(Kelly laughing)

00:01:47.092 --> 00:01:48.622
- You know, the only time-
- That means yes!

00:01:48.622 --> 00:01:49.522
(Kelly laughing)

00:01:49.522 --> 00:01:50.355
(people chattering)

00:01:50.355 --> 00:01:52.095
- You both by the way went,

00:01:52.095 --> 00:01:52.928
mm?

00:01:52.928 --> 00:01:53.761
(people laughing)

00:01:53.761 --> 00:01:55.099
- And we were like,
should we tell the story?

00:01:55.099 --> 00:01:56.130
(faintly speaking)

00:01:56.130 --> 00:01:57.570
But I do think on this record,

00:01:57.570 --> 00:01:59.670
there was one song in
particular that stood out to us

00:01:59.670 --> 00:02:01.710
that I knew was like, this
is going on the record.

00:02:01.710 --> 00:02:02.880
- Yeah.
- And Aly was like,

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:04.770
I don't know
- I don't know.

00:02:04.770 --> 00:02:07.830
- It feels a little bit soft
compared to the other songs.

00:02:07.830 --> 00:02:09.450
It's a little more gospel leaning.

00:02:09.450 --> 00:02:10.410
But like, we also know that

00:02:10.410 --> 00:02:11.760
that's kind of where our roots came from.

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:12.593
So we were like, let's honor this.

00:02:12.593 --> 00:02:13.426
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:02:13.426 --> 00:02:14.259
- But Aly was like, I'm not sure

00:02:14.259 --> 00:02:15.092
if it works on this album.
- I don't know,

00:02:15.092 --> 00:02:15.925
I was second guessing it.

00:02:15.925 --> 00:02:16.800
- I was like, this is my CBA.
- Which every artist does.

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:18.090
- This song is going on it.
- Yeah, everybody does, yeah.

00:02:18.090 --> 00:02:19.140
- And thank God because-

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:22.080
- And now, I mean, it makes
sense that it's on the record

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:23.467
and I'm glad that AJ pulled it.

00:02:23.467 --> 00:02:24.300
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:25.440
- Pulled the CBA out.
- Yeah.

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:26.273
- Usually that's how it works.
- But I think all artists

00:02:26.273 --> 00:02:27.106
do that.

00:02:27.106 --> 00:02:28.470
We second guess things.
- Yeah.

00:02:28.470 --> 00:02:29.303
- And you can't ever predict

00:02:29.303 --> 00:02:30.600
what people are gonna like anyway,

00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:31.920
so you might as well
just pick what you love

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:32.753
and put it out.
- It's so true.

00:02:32.753 --> 00:02:35.340
- I wonder if The Beatles
would still have been together

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:36.260
if they had a CBA.

00:02:36.260 --> 00:02:37.097
- [Kelly] There you go.

00:02:37.097 --> 00:02:38.931
- That is such a great point.
- Great point.

00:02:38.931 --> 00:02:39.866
- You know.
- If they had a CBA.

00:02:39.866 --> 00:02:41.553
- If The Beatles had a CBA.

00:02:41.553 --> 00:02:46.170
- It's a slightly woman thing
of recognizing someone's,

00:02:46.170 --> 00:02:47.077
and guys, I'm sorry to be,

00:02:47.077 --> 00:02:49.386
you know.
- I agree with you.

00:02:49.386 --> 00:02:50.632
- See how womanly I can be.

00:02:50.632 --> 00:02:51.990
- Yeah. Yeah.
- Yeah, you can, you can.

00:02:51.990 --> 00:02:55.530
- They lock horns and if
they had this, you know,

00:02:55.530 --> 00:02:58.080
maybe some of the great
bands would still be together

00:02:58.080 --> 00:02:59.130
that broke up.

00:02:59.130 --> 00:03:00.930
- Well, maybe we're changing
history right here, guys.

00:03:00.930 --> 00:03:02.940
- Yes, there you go. You're welcome.

00:03:02.940 --> 00:03:04.020
You're welcome

00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:05.622
- You're welcome.
Everyone gets a CBA today.

00:03:05.622 --> 00:03:06.455
- Yeah. You get a CBA, you get a CBA.

00:03:06.455 --> 00:03:08.400
- They're like, okay,
what do I do with this?

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:10.260
- I'm gonna use that with my kids.

00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:12.870
But they're not allowed to
have one. (everyone laughing)

00:03:12.870 --> 00:03:15.090
I'm just kidding. It's a democracy.

00:03:15.090 --> 00:03:17.100
So tell us about this song
you're gonna be performing.

00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:19.680
It's called "Blue Dress."
What's this song about?

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:23.082
- Well, when we got in to write
music for this new record,

00:03:23.082 --> 00:03:24.480
for whatever reason,

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:26.700
we felt like we were
really connected to like,

00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:28.260
old country songs at that moment.

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:30.570
I mean, we always have grown
up listening to country music,

00:03:30.570 --> 00:03:32.910
like The Judds and,
you know, Loretta Lynn,

00:03:32.910 --> 00:03:35.657
and these great country
artists and these songs

00:03:35.657 --> 00:03:39.000
that have this incredible
storytelling behind them.

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:41.790
And we wanted to kind of encapsulate that

00:03:41.790 --> 00:03:42.623
within this song.

00:03:42.623 --> 00:03:45.120
And you know, we're not legends like them

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:46.294
but, you know-
- No, but that's the-

00:03:46.294 --> 00:03:47.580
- They inspire us.

00:03:47.580 --> 00:03:48.960
- Yeah. And the storytelling

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:50.500
of those artists you just mentioned too.

00:03:50.500 --> 00:03:51.360
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:52.193
- Love that.

00:03:52.193 --> 00:03:55.170
So Aly and AJ have a
surprise for everyone here.

00:03:55.170 --> 00:03:57.150
This spring they're
actually headlining a tour

00:03:57.150 --> 00:03:59.190
that'll take them to some
of the most amazing venues

00:03:59.190 --> 00:04:00.023
in the nation,

00:04:00.023 --> 00:04:01.296
like Nashville's Ryman Auditorium.

00:04:01.296 --> 00:04:03.420
Oh my God, I love singing there.

00:04:03.420 --> 00:04:04.590
Los Angeles, Greek Theater.

00:04:04.590 --> 00:04:05.670
What a great place to play.

00:04:05.670 --> 00:04:06.690
I love watching a show there too.

00:04:06.690 --> 00:04:09.060
And New York's Hammerstein Ballroom.

00:04:09.060 --> 00:04:09.893
I love that one as well.

00:04:09.893 --> 00:04:10.726
You're playing all the cool places.

00:04:10.726 --> 00:04:12.180
- Thanks.
- I love this tour.

00:04:12.180 --> 00:04:13.590
- We're really excited for this tour.

00:04:13.590 --> 00:04:15.240
- [Kelly] It does matter. The venue.

00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:16.440
- It does matter.
- It totally matters.

00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:17.880
- I didn't know that when I was younger.

00:04:17.880 --> 00:04:18.713
- Neither did we.

00:04:18.713 --> 00:04:21.056
- Yeah, and then you learn
like, wait, like the vibe,

00:04:21.056 --> 00:04:23.930
the acoustics, it matters.
- It does.

00:04:23.930 --> 00:04:25.890
- Do you feel like that
when y'all are on stage

00:04:25.890 --> 00:04:26.723
or on set or anything?

00:04:26.723 --> 00:04:27.900
The environment really affects you?

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:29.040
- I've never been on stage,

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:30.667
I've never done a play.
- But even on set,

00:04:30.667 --> 00:04:32.332
like when you're on set
and you're (indistinct)

00:04:32.332 --> 00:04:33.165
and they have this set built out-

00:04:33.165 --> 00:04:34.290
- I think the environment,

00:04:34.290 --> 00:04:36.090
yeah, the environment obviously.

00:04:36.090 --> 00:04:38.400
What goes on outside the set,

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:39.540
you know, the temperature,

00:04:39.540 --> 00:04:40.800
the kind of place you're in,

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:42.219
I think can influence your work.

00:04:42.219 --> 00:04:44.550
- Location even.
- Yeah, location, you know.

00:04:44.550 --> 00:04:46.410
- Yeah, like (indistinct).
- Miserable place but-

00:04:46.410 --> 00:04:49.290
- I mean, acting's funny
'cause you've got to shut out

00:04:49.290 --> 00:04:50.490
so much, you know?
- Yeah.

00:04:50.490 --> 00:04:51.756
- Where even in the best of circumstances-

00:04:51.756 --> 00:04:52.828
- Yeah.
- You're blocking out so much.

00:04:52.828 --> 00:04:53.940
(Helen faintly speaking)
- Yeah.

00:04:53.940 --> 00:04:56.070
So it's a little different,

00:04:56.070 --> 00:04:57.810
but I suppose the less
you have to shut out,

00:04:57.810 --> 00:04:59.280
and especially if you're
doing a longer take,

00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:01.410
and sometimes the camera's
far across the street

00:05:01.410 --> 00:05:02.910
then it starts to really cook.

00:05:03.821 --> 00:05:04.680
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

00:05:04.680 --> 00:05:06.540
- And most certainly as theater acting,

00:05:06.540 --> 00:05:07.915
the venue absolutely counts,

00:05:07.915 --> 00:05:09.030
hugely.
- Yeah, I agree.

00:05:09.030 --> 00:05:10.594
- The acoustics number one, you know,

00:05:10.594 --> 00:05:12.390
but also just the structure

00:05:12.390 --> 00:05:14.752
of where the audience are.
- That's where architecture

00:05:14.752 --> 00:05:15.585
really does play in.
- You know,

00:05:15.585 --> 00:05:16.418
it's incredibly important.

00:05:16.418 --> 00:05:17.398
- [Kelly] The acoustics
and how it was built.

00:05:17.398 --> 00:05:18.231
- I couldn't agree more with that.

00:05:18.231 --> 00:05:19.650
- Yeah. We don't think about that anymore,

00:05:19.650 --> 00:05:21.240
but it does play a part.

00:05:21.240 --> 00:05:23.070
Well, everyone here will have a chance

00:05:23.070 --> 00:05:25.650
to join Aly and AJ on tour

00:05:25.650 --> 00:05:28.080
because they are each getting two tickets

00:05:28.080 --> 00:05:33.080
to a show of your choice.
(audience screaming)

00:05:33.124 --> 00:05:34.361
We are very excited!

